ny of you guys watching probably  aren't even 17 years old if you invested  in the s p 500 instead you would have  had a better time but not by much  first you would have experienced a 50  crash along with the seven year long way  to return to all-time highs and adjust  when you think things are finally going  bullish again you're hit with the 57  crash that takes you to lower levels in  the dot-com crash and it's not until 13  years after your initial investment that  you finally start to make money so as  you can see if you go all in at the  wrong time you could still easily be  stuck for well over a decade even with  an index fund but at least you can be  sure that index funds will recover  sooner or later  right well michael berry seems to think  that index funds are actually in some  sort of crazy bubble themselves due to  the rise of passive investing and that  index funds are doomed to crash and not  recover but that's a whole other  discussion  so far we've gone through several  examples and hypotheticals but the  lesson at the end of the day is to just  not fomo into the market just because  the market dips 10 or 15 or 20 percent  doesn't mean that you should bet the  farm on an index or any individual stock  this type of behavior is what leads to  people losing money presenting the stock  market and missing out on the best  buying opportunity of the decade by the  time the stock market actually bottoms  they've either stopped investing or they  ran out of sears capital a long time ago  because they just kept buying it every  dip so what should you do it instead  well this is not financial advice but  the smartest course of action in my  opinion is simply a long-term weighted  dollar cost averaging strategy maybe  every week you invest 100 into the s p  500 this way regardless of what the  market is doing it you have at least  some money going into the market this is  extremely important because the only  risk that's bigger than following in at  the top is 